   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Is there some way to produce a directory of the .stan files that rstanarm uses? Do they live somewhere outside of the package?<div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "42ayRD4yBQAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-06-17T22:18:45Z",
   "subject": "Re: rstanarm .stan models",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, June 17, 2016 at 5:31:57 PM UTC-4, Krzysztof Sakrejda wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Is there some way to produce a directory of the .stan files that rstanarm uses? Do they live somewhere outside of the package?</div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>They live in the exec/ directory<br><br><a href=\"https://github.com/stan-dev/rstanarm/tree/master/exec\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/stan-dev/rstanarm/tree/master/exec&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792461171837000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3EQ-Vnk1nwRDrPvrUqpV8s\">https://github.com/stan-dev/rstanarm/tree/master/exec</a><br><br>but utilize the #include mechanism a lot. You could do<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">sapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rstanarm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stanmodels</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FUN </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  cat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m@model_code</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> file </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> paste0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;/tmp/&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> m@model_name</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;.stan&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sep </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;\\n&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  invisible</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NULL</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">})</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>to write Stan files into /tmp after the #include lines have been resolved.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
